  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mostly dry and comfortable weather is expected through\nLabor Day. The exception will be near coastal New Jersey and \nDelaware where some showers are possible today. \n\nA drier and cooler airmass has arrived across the northern Middle \nAtlantic region as high pressure to the northwest begins influencing \nour weather to a greater degree. Low pressure aloft across the \nNorth Atlantic continues to bring northwest flow aloft over our \narea. A couple embedded shortwaves in this flow will pass \nacross New England today. This will result in some isolated to\nscattered showers near the New Jersey and Delaware coasts\nthrough the afternoon. Pops are generally in the 15-30% range, \nso no significant impacts are expected. Otherwise, partly cloudy\nskies are expected with highs in the mid 70s to low 80s. Similar\ntemperatures will follow for Labor Day with more sun than \nclouds and dry weather.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Minor tidal flooding is anticipated with this \nafternoon's high tide, mainly for the southern Jersey Shore, \nDelaware Beaches, and Delaware Bay communities. A Coastal Flood \nAdvisory remains in effect from 3 PM to 9 PM.\n\nFlow will continue to turn more onshore today. This will result in \nwater piling up over consecutive tide cycles, leading to minor tidal \nflooding across portions of our area this afternoon.\nThe current forecast has minor tidal flooding from Atlantic City on \nsouth along the Atlantic Ocean coastline and for areas within \nDelaware Bay. A Coastal Flood Advisory remains in effect for \nAtlantic, Cape May, and Cumberland County in NJ and Kent and Sussex \nCounty in Delaware from 3 PM this afternoon until 9 PM this evening\n\nWithin the tidal Delaware River, northern NJ coast, and Chesapeake \nBay, tidal flooding will be limited to spotty minor or no flooding \nat all. Tide levels should decrease getting into next week as flow \nturns more offshore.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A cold front will cross the area Thursday\nbringing the potential for another round of showers and storms.\n\nMid-level ridging will be in place across central and northeastern \nportions of the US through Tuesday, with high pressure also in \ncontrol at the surface. The result will be tranquil conditions. \nStarting on Wednesday, ridging aloft is expected to begin to break \ndown as an upper-low tracks eastward from portions of Ontario into \nportions of Quebec. Current guidance has height falls beginning \nacross the area on Wednesday, with a strong shortwave pivoting \nthrough the region on Thursday. At the surface, high pressure will \nshift offshore Wednesday, with a cold front moving through sometime \non Thursday.\n\nWednesday into Thursday, as the high moves offshore, a warm and \nmoist southerly flow develops across the area, with highs warming \ninto the mid-upper 80s for many, and dewpoints rising into the \nmid/upper 60s or low 70s for Delmarva and south NJ. \n\nIt is not entirely out of the question that as the ridge begins to \nbreak down some shower and storm chances could ultimately evolve \nacross portions of the area Wednesday night. We currently feature \nPoPs generally in the 20-40% range for this time period across the \nSouthern Poconos and northern NJ. Slightly better shower and storm \nchances are likely to exist across the area on Thursday when the \nactual cold front moves through the region. At this point, PoPs Thursday\nare also generally in the 30-50% range, but would not be surprised\nif they trend up some in the coming days. Currently, the threat\nfor severe storms or flash flooding concerns appears low.\n\nFollowing the system, a period of fair weather and seasonable \ntemperatures will be across the area Friday and next weekend.",
